21:33:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A successful transplant operation means the organ was connected properly and is working initially—it does **not** necessarily mean the immune system has permanently accepted it. The body can reject a transplant: - **Immediately or within hours** if there are pre-existing antibodies against the donor. - **Days to months later** as immune cells recognize the donor tissue as foreign. - **Over years** through slow, chronic immune damage. That is why most organ-transplant recipients need anti-rejection medication for as long as the transplanted organ is in place. With good donor matching and modern immunosuppressive drugs, many transplants can function for years or decades, but the donor organ still has different DNA and remains capable of triggering immune attack. There are exceptions: tissue transplanted from the same person—such as skin moved from one area to another—has the same DNA and is usually accepted without anti-rejection drugs.
