21:33:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s great—many kidney transplants do work extremely well for many years, especially with a good donor match, careful follow-up, and anti-rejection medication. Your uncle’s experience is completely consistent with modern transplant medicine: his immune system may be well controlled and the kidney may be a good immunologic match, so he has had no *clinical* rejection episodes. But that success is usually not because the body treats the kidney as genetically identical. Rather, the immune response is prevented or kept low enough that the organ can function normally. Most kidney recipients take long-term immunosuppressants even when they feel perfectly well, and doctors monitor kidney function and sometimes donor-specific antibodies because rejection can be silent at first. A transplant can therefore be both “accepted in practice” and still biologically foreign to the immune system.
