21:33:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The tree-graft analogy is useful for the **physical** side of transplantation: tissues can heal together, establish blood flow, and function as one system. But tree grafts also require compatibility. A branch usually grafts successfully only onto a closely related rootstock, and incompatible grafts can fail over time. Plants do not have the same adaptive immune system humans do, with antibodies and specialized immune cells trained to attack foreign tissue. In humans, an organ can heal into place and work well, but its cells still display donor-specific markers. Unless donor and recipient are an unusually close immune match, the recipient’s immune system can react against those markers. Anti-rejection medication is what allows many successful transplants—such as your uncle’s kidney—to remain healthy long-term. So bodies are built to heal injuries and integrate connected tissue, but not generally to ignore a genetically different organ without help.
